    "created_at": "2023-08-14T19:51:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-08-14T19:51:56Z",
    "body": "> The `=` restriction is presumably for the case where you just didn't put a space between your attributes, like `<a id=foohref=bar>`, but that seems less likely to be a problem?\r\n\r\nIndeed, that's why it's a parse error. It helps catching such mistakes.\r\n\r\n[Data from validator.nu](https://htmlparser.info/conformance-checkers/#most-common-errors) from a few years ago:\r\n\r\n- \"X\" in an unquoted attribute value. Probable causes: Attributes running together or a URL query string in an unquoted attribute value. 1.39%\r\n\r\nFor comparison:\r\n\r\n- No space between attributes. 6.69%\r\n- Saw \"<\" when expecting an attribute name. Probable cause: \">\" missing immediately before. 1.25%\r\n\r\nI think `=` should continue to be an error because the original reason is still valid.",
